---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../components/ComparisonTable.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import CtaSection from '../components/CtaSection.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';

const title = `Why ${site.brand}`;
const description = `How ${site.brand} compares to Strimzi, AWS MSK, and Confluent Cloud — feature-by-feature, with the honest gaps.`;

const breadcrumbs = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${site.url}/` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Why', item: `${site.url}/why/` },
  ],
};
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={breadcrumbs}>
  <section class="container-site py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl text-center">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Why {site.brand}</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-5xl leading-tight tracking-tight md:text-6xl">
        The honest comparison.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-fg-muted">
        Strimzi, AWS MSK, and Confluent Cloud are excellent products. They're
        also designed around different assumptions. Here's where {site.brand}
        is genuinely different, and where the alternatives are the right call.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-site pb-16">
    <ComparisonTable />
  </section>

  <!-- Pillar 1 -->
  <section class="container-site grid grid-cols-1 gap-12 py-20 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">01 · Multi-cluster KRaft</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-4xl tracking-tight">One quorum across many K8s clusters.</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-fg-muted">
        Strimzi, MSK, and Confluent each run a Kafka cluster inside one
        Kubernetes cluster (or one cloud region). {site.brand} runs <strong>one
        Kafka cluster across three or more Kubernetes clusters</strong> via
        any MCS-compatible mesh (Submariner Lighthouse, Cilium Cluster Mesh,
        Istio multi-cluster). Controllers form a single KRaft quorum;
        brokers stay local for low-latency client access.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
        That means losing an entire Kubernetes cluster — region, zone, or
        provider — doesn't lose your Kafka quorum. There's no DR cutover
        because there's no DR.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">
        <li>· Controllers mesh via <code class="font-mono text-fg">svc.clusterset.local</code></li>
        <li>· Declarative cross-cluster roll ordering</li>
        <li>· ServiceExport for proxy / UI / registry</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <CodeBlock filename="kafka-cluster.yaml" lang="yaml" code={`spec:
  clusters:
    - id: A
      controllerAdvertisedAddress: controllers-a-headless.kafka.svc.clusterset.local:9093
    - id: B
      controllerAdvertisedAddress: controllers-b-headless.kafka.svc.clusterset.local:9093
    - id: C
      controllerAdvertisedAddress: controllers-c-headless.kafka.svc.clusterset.local:9093
  clusterRollOrder: ["A", "B", "C"]
`} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Pillar 2 -->
  <section class="container-site grid grid-cols-1 gap-12 py-20 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="lg:order-2">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">02 · Wire-level RBAC</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-4xl tracking-tight">OIDC, JWT groups, topic + schema ACLs.</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-fg-muted">
        {site.brand} ships <a class="text-brand-bright underline" href="https://kroxylicious.io">Kroxylicious</a> as a first-class CRD with
        custom filters that validate JWTs and enforce <code class="font-mono text-fg">KafkaRbac</code> rules.
        Topic and schema-registry ACLs live in the same CR; the same JWT covers
        both client and registry access.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
        Strimzi can be wired to Keycloak via the authorizer, but you write and
        maintain the policy stack yourself. {site.brand} ships the policy
        engine as part of the operator.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="lg:order-1">
      <CodeBlock filename="kafka-rbac.yaml" lang="yaml" code={`spec:
  groups:
    - name: orders-team
      kafka:
        topics: [orders, orders-dlq]
        operations: [PRODUCE, FETCH]
      schemaRegistry:
        artifacts: [orders]
        actions: [READ, WRITE]
    - name: schema-admin
      schemaRegistry:
        artifacts: ["*"]
        actions: [READ, WRITE, DELETE]
`} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Pillar 3 -->
  <section class="container-site grid grid-cols-1 gap-12 py-20 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">03 · Schemas that survive mirroring</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-4xl tracking-tight">MirrorMaker2 that actually mirrors schemas.</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-fg-muted">
        Mirroring topics across clusters is solved. Mirroring <strong>Apicurio
        V3 envelope global IDs</strong> so consumers can decode the payloads is
        not — until now. {site.brand} ships a Kafka Connect SMT
        (<code class="font-mono text-fg">ApicurioSchemaTransferSmt</code>) that
        rewrites global IDs per record and recursively resolves schema references.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
        Both Strimzi MM2 and Confluent Replicator require manual schema-ID coordination.
        {site.brand} doesn't.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <CodeBlock filename="mm2.yaml" lang="yaml" code={`spec:
  source:
    clusterRef: prod-eu
    schemaRegistry:
      url: http://apicurio-eu.kafka.svc.cluster.local:8080
  target:
    clusterRef: prod-us
    schemaRegistry:
      url: http://apicurio-us.kafka.svc.cluster.local:8080
  schemaTransfer:
    enabled: true   # rewrites global IDs per record
`} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Pillar 4 -->
  <section class="container-site grid grid-cols-1 gap-12 py-20 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="lg:order-2">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">04 · Backup that ships with the platform</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-4xl tracking-tight">Declarative backup &amp; PITR restore.</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-fg-muted">
        A <code class="font-mono text-fg">KafkaBackup</code> CR builds a CronJob that
        streams records and offsets to S3 / Azure / GCS / PVC. <code class="font-mono text-fg">KafkaRestore</code> is
        idempotent with topic remapping and point-in-time recovery. Schemas can
        be exported alongside.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
        Strimzi has no first-party backup. MSK has snapshots but no PITR. {site.brand}
        gives you both, in YAML.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="lg:order-1">
      <CodeBlock filename="kafka-backup.yaml" lang="yaml" code={`spec:
  clusterRef: prod
  schedule: "0 */6 * * *"
  destination: s3://kafka-backups/prod
  includeSchemas: true
  retention:
    days: 30
`} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- When NOT to choose -->
  <section class="container-site py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl rounded-2xl border border-border bg-bg-raised/60 p-10">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">When you should pick something else</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl tracking-tight">We're not always the answer.</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-fg-muted">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-fg">If you just need single-cluster Kafka, choose Strimzi.</strong>
          It's mature, well-staffed, and the right shape for that problem.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-fg">If you're all-in on AWS and want zero ops, choose MSK Serverless.</strong>
          You give up Kubernetes-native control; you get an SLA.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-fg">If you want a hosted control plane today, choose Confluent Cloud.</strong>
          Our hosted product isn't shipped yet — talk to us about early access.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-fg">If you need a single broker in a dev cluster, use <code class="font-mono">kafka-native</code>.</strong>
          An operator is overkill.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaSection />
</Base>
